 Understanding the SPI Waveform

The waveform represents various SPI signals over time (in nanoseconds). The key signals shown are:
•	clk (Clock) → System clock toggling at regular intervals.
•	cs (Chip Select) → Goes LOW when communication is active.
•	miso (Master In, Slave Out) → Data received from the slave.
•	mosi (Master Out, Slave In) → Data sent from the master.
•	reset → Initializes the system before communication starts.
•	sck (SPI Clock) → Controls timing for data transfer.

🔍 Key Observations
1.	Clock (clk & sck) Behavior
   o	clk toggles continuously.
   o	sck changes on every rising edge of clk, synchronizing SPI data.

2.	Chip Select (cs) Activation
   o	cs starts HIGH → No communication.
   o	When cs goes LOW → SPI master starts data transfer.

3.	MOSI & MISO Data Flow
   o	When mosi = HIGH (1), the master sends 1 to the slave.
   o	miso mirrors mosi, demonstrating a simple loopback.

4.	Reset Signal Impact
   o	reset is HIGH initially, keeping SPI inactive.
   o	reset goes LOW → SPI operation begins.
